    "image_with_text_JPhWc9": {
      "type": "image-with-text",
      "blocks": {
        "heading_XAjYEy": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Paper Tape Applications",
            "highlight_style": "none",
            "highlight_font_style": "italic",
            "highlight_style_color": "",
            "highlight_text_color": "",
            "heading_size": "h2"
          }
        },
        "text_YnRQzt": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Paper tape is commonly used for general purpose bonding, bonding of irregular surfaces, and applications where temporary holds are required. It’s compatible with a variety of surface materials, including paper, corrugated cardboard, fabric, plastic, and film. Many types can be torn by hand during application.</p><p>Paper tapes are used for color separation when hand painting or spray painting to create crisp, clean lines and prevent overspray. They’re common elements in many manufacturing processes, including furniture making, <a href=\"/pages/automotive\" title=\"Automotive\">automobile</a> and aircraft painting, appliance manufacturing, decorative glassmaking, powder coating, plating, and sandblasting. Many industries also use paper tape for <a href=\"/pages/eco-friendly-labels-and-sustainability-practices\" title=\"Eco-Friendly Labels & Sustainability Practices\">sustainable packaging</a> and because it’s <a href=\"/pages/tamper-evident-labels\" title=\"Tamper-Evident Labels\">tamper-evident</a>.</p>",
            "subtext_color": true
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "heading_XAjYEy",
        "text_YnRQzt"
      ],
